# Workout Tracker: Technical Design & Implementation Plan

Last updated: 2026-11-13

## Overview

Workout Tracker is a local web app that builds a varied stretch-and-strength routine from the user's own 233 exercises, walks through it one exercise at a time, and logs what was done. It runs on one laptop and is cast to the TV from a browser tab. It is for personal use only, so there are no accounts, no hosting and no cost.

The app never decides which exercises exist: the user does, in two CSV files. The app only decides which subset to show today, and it remembers what was completed so every exercise comes around before any repeats.

The goal is overall health improvement with variety. The build should get something working quickly and double as a refresher in Python and Flask.

## Requirements

The app must generate a different workout on each training day, guide the user through it with images and timers, and record what happened. It must do this on a single laptop with no recurring cost.

**Functional**

- **Library:** 233 exercises (114 stretch, 119 strength) loaded from two CSV files and browsable in the app.
- **Schedule:** Monday, Wednesday and Friday are full days (10 stretches, 10 strength, 10 different stretches). Tuesday and Thursday are 20 stretches including 3 Balance. Saturday and Sunday are rest days with a "start anyway" option.
- **Selection:** every body area appears at least once per block, and every exercise is used before any repeats (see Workout selection).
- **Guided flow:** one exercise at a time showing name, description, dose and image. Space moves to the next one.
- **Timer:** an on-screen countdown for stretches, driven by a `seconds` value; it runs twice when the stretch is done per side.
- **Logging:** for each session, the date, which exercises were done or skipped, and an optional note. Strength exercises also log sets, reps and weight.
- **Backup:** a copy of the database file in V1; CSV export in V2.

**Non-functional**

- Laptop only (no mobile). Internet is needed only to load images.
- Free to run, with as few dependencies as possible: Flask, plus pytest for tests.
- Plain, readable code, built step by step in the terminal.

**Decisions already made**

| Topic | Decision |
| --- | --- |
| Exercise source | One-time export from the Google Sheet to two CSV files; no live sync |
| Stretch vs strength | Taken from which CSV the row is in, not from a column |
| Balance | A separate area, kept apart from Whole body; only on Tuesday and Thursday |
| Combined area label | "Hips & Glutes; Thighs" becomes "Hips & Glutes" |
| Extra slots | Go first to Hips & Glutes, Thighs and Back, then spread across the rest |
| Images | Hotlinked from the web; broken links are acceptable in V1 |
| Weekends | Rest days |
| Backup | CSV export is V2 |

## Scope

Version 1 is everything needed to do a real workout and keep a record of it; anything that only reads that record back in a nicer way waits for V2.

**In V1**

- CSV import, SQLite storage and the exercise library page
- Day-type schedule and the selection logic
- The one-exercise-at-a-time workout screen with Space, Skip, Back and notes
- Stretch countdown timer, including the per-side case
- Strength logging of sets, reps and weight
- A per-session summary and a plain history list
- A backup script that copies the database file

**V2 ideas**

- Calendar view and progress summaries
- CSV export as the backup format
- Showing the last logged sets and weight beside each strength exercise
- Downloading images locally so the app works offline
- Editing exercises inside the app
- A phone-friendly layout

**Non-goals**

- Any AI component: selection is plain rules plus history
- Accounts, hosting, or syncing across computers
- Live sync with Google Sheets
- Public release

## Architecture and tech stack

A single Flask process on localhost serves server-rendered pages backed by one SQLite file, with a little vanilla JavaScript driving the workout screen. Nothing runs in the cloud, so there is no hosting cost and no login.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  Browser["Browser tab<br/>cast to the TV<br/>pages + workout.js<br/>keys, timer, set logging"] <-->|HTTP| Flask
  Images["Image hosts<br/>demo images from the web"] -->|loads images| Browser
  subgraph Flask["Flask app on localhost"]
    App["app.py<br/>routes and JSON API"]
    Sel["selection.py<br/>the core: picks workouts, no DB"]
    DB["db.py<br/>schema and history queries"]
  end
  Import["import_data.py<br/>reads both CSV files"] -->|upserts| SQLite
  DB <-->|SQL| SQLite[("SQLite file<br/>data/workout.db")]
```

The browser talks only to Flask, which reads and writes one SQLite file; the importer fills the same file, and the browser fetches images straight from their sites.

| Piece | Choice | Why |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Backend | Python and Flask | Already familiar, fastest route to a working version |
| Database | SQLite through Python's `sqlite3`, no ORM | One file, nothing to install, backup is a file copy |
| Pages | Jinja templates | Server-rendered, no build step |
| Browser code | Vanilla JavaScript | Space key, timer and saving need very little code |
| Styling | One CSS file, dark theme, large type | Readable from the couch on a TV |
| Tests | pytest | The selection logic is pure functions, easy to test |
| TV | Casting the browser tab | The app is just a web page |

C# with ASP.NET would be better practice, but the stated goal is speed. The schema and selection rules would port over cleanly later.

## Source data and import

Two CSV files are the source of truth for exercises, and a re-runnable import loads them into SQLite. The counts below are from the current work-in-progress sheets.

| Area | Stretch | Strength |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Hips & Glutes | 33 | 17 |
| Thighs | 28 | 21 |
| Back | 12 | 11 |
| Balance | 8 | 0 |
| Shoulders | 7 | 13 |
| Lower legs & Feet | 6 | 5 |
| Neck | 6 | 0 |
| Whole body | 6 | 10 |
| Chest | 3 | 8 |
| Core | 3 | 22 |
| Arms & hands | 2 | 12 |
| **Total** | **114** | **119** |

The stretch count of Hips & Glutes includes the two rows originally labelled "Hips & Glutes; Thighs". The stretch file has two extra columns, `seconds` and `per_side`, plus a temporary `prefill_review` column that the importer ignores.

**Import rules**

1. The exercise type comes from the file (`stretch.csv` or `strength.csv`), not from a column.
2. Rows with no area or no name are skipped and counted. The raw sheet exports contain hundreds of blank padding rows.
3. Whitespace is trimmed, and area names that differ only by capitalization collapse to one spelling.
4. An area like "Hips & Glutes; Thighs" keeps only the first area.
5. `on_floor`, `per_side` and `seconds` are validated. A blank floor flag defaults to false and a missing dose is imported, each with a warning.
6. Equipment that is blank or starts with "None" means no equipment; anything else is shown as a short note on screen.
7. Re-running the import updates rows matched on type and name. Exercises that disappear from a CSV are marked inactive, never deleted, so history survives. Renaming an exercise creates a new one.
8. The import ends with a report: rows per type and area, rows skipped, and warnings.

## Data model

Four tables hold everything: the exercise library, one row per workout session, one row per exercise in a session, and one row per strength set. Rotation counts are derived from `session_items` and never stored, so there is one source of truth.

```sql
CREATE TABLE exercises (
  id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,
  type TEXT NOT NULL CHECK (type IN ('stretch','strength')),
  focus TEXT NOT NULL,
  name TEXT NOT NULL,
  description TEXT,
  dose TEXT,
  image_url TEXT,
  equipment TEXT,
  on_floor INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,
  seconds INTEGER,
  per_side INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,
  active INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 1,
  UNIQUE (type, name)
);

CREATE TABLE sessions (
  id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,
  date TEXT NOT NULL,
  day_type TEXT NOT NULL CHECK (day_type IN ('full','flex')),
  started_at TEXT NOT NULL,
  finished_at TEXT
);

CREATE TABLE session_items (
  id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,
  session_id INTEGER NOT NULL REFERENCES sessions(id),
  position INTEGER NOT NULL,
  block TEXT NOT NULL,
  exercise_id INTEGER NOT NULL REFERENCES exercises(id),
  status TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT 'pending'
    CHECK (status IN ('pending','done','skipped')),
  note TEXT,
  completed_at TEXT
);

CREATE TABLE strength_sets (
  id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,
  session_item_id INTEGER NOT NULL REFERENCES session_items(id),
  set_number INTEGER NOT NULL,
  reps INTEGER,
  weight REAL
);
```

- **Dates and times** are stored as ISO text (`2026-11-13`, `2026-11-13T14:16:00`).
- **Blocks** are `warmup`, `strength` and `cooldown` on full days, and `stretch` on flexibility days.
- **Times done** for an exercise is the count of its `session_items` rows with status `done`; **last done** is the latest `completed_at` of those rows. Skipped items count toward neither.
- **Weight** is stored as a number in pounds; the unit is a single setting.
- SQLite needs `PRAGMA foreign_keys = ON` on every connection to enforce the references.

## Workout selection

Selection is a set of pure functions in `selection.py`: given the exercises, the history and a random generator, they return an ordered list. They touch no database and no clock, which makes them easy to test.

| Day | Type | Plan |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Mon, Wed, Fri | Full | 10 stretches, 10 strength, 10 different stretches |
| Tue, Thu | Flex | 20 stretches: 3 Balance plus 17 from the other areas |
| Sat, Sun | Rest | Rest message, with "start anyway" for either type |

**How a block is built**

1. Start from the active exercises of the block's type. On full days drop Balance, and drop anything already picked earlier in the session.
2. Read the history: times done per exercise, and the most recent done date per area.
3. Give every area that has a candidate one slot. If there are fewer slots than areas, the areas that have gone longest without work win.
4. Hand out extra slots one at a time: first to Hips & Glutes, Thighs and Back (longest gap first), then to the remaining areas (longest gap first), cycling until the slots run out. An area never gets more slots than it has candidates.
5. Inside each area, rank exercises by times done (fewest first), then by last done (never done, then oldest), then randomly, and take the top ones.
6. Order the picks so the same area is not back to back, with standing exercises and floor exercises grouped.
7. On Tuesday and Thursday, pick the 3 Balance exercises with the same ranking and merge them in before ordering.

A skipped exercise is not counted as done, so it keeps its place at the front of its area's queue. This is what makes the 6 Neck exercises each come up once before any repeat, while Hips & Glutes keeps serving ones it has not shown yet.

**Slot examples**

| Block | Slots | Areas | Result |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Stretch block, full day | 10 | 10 | One each |
| Strength block, full day | 10 | 9 | One each, plus one extra to whichever of Hips & Glutes, Thighs or Back has gone longest |
| Stretches without Balance, flex day | 17 | 10 | One each, one extra each to Hips & Glutes, Thighs and Back, then one extra to the 4 other areas with the longest gap |

**Order on a full day.** Warm-up stretches go standing then floor, strength goes floor then standing, and cool-down stretches go standing then floor. That is 3 floor-to-standing changes across 30 exercises, one fewer than ending the cool-down standing. The second stretch block excludes everything in the first, so all 20 stretches differ.

## App behavior

The app has a home page, a library, a workout screen and a history list, and the workout screen is built to be driven entirely from the keyboard while casting to the TV.

| Route | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `GET /` | Today's day type, Start or Resume, and "start anyway" on rest days |
| `POST /start` | Build a session from the selection plan and open it |
| `GET /session/<id>` | The one-exercise-at-a-time workout screen |
| `POST /api/items/<id>` | Save status, note and strength sets for one exercise |
| `POST /session/<id>/finish` | Mark the session finished |
| `GET /session/<id>/summary` | Done and skipped exercises with sets |
| `GET /exercises` | The full library with times done and last done |
| `GET /history` | Sessions listed by date |

**Workout screen.** Each exercise shows its position (for example 7 of 30), block, name, body area, a floor or standing tag, description, dose text, any equipment note, and the image. Images load with no referrer, which avoids some hotlink blocking, and fall back to a text placeholder if they fail. The page uses a dark theme and large type for the TV.

| Key | Action |
| --- | --- |
| Space | Mark done and go to the next exercise |
| S | Skip and go to the next exercise |
| Left arrow | Go back one exercise |
| Enter | Start or pause the timer |
| N | Jump to the note field |
| A | Add a set row (strength) |
| Esc | Leave a text field |

Space is ignored while typing in a field, so notes and numbers are safe.

**Timer.** A stretch with a `seconds` value shows a countdown. If it is marked per side, the timer runs twice with a switch-sides prompt between runs. A short beep from the browser marks the end. The timer starts on Enter rather than automatically, and it never advances the screen on its own.

**Strength.** Strength exercises show rows for set, reps and weight.

**Saving.** Status is saved when Space or S is pressed. Notes and sets are saved when their field loses focus. If the tab closes mid-workout, Resume opens the first exercise still pending.

## Project structure

The project is a flat folder of a few small Python modules, with the data, templates and tests in their own directories.

```text
workout-tracker/
├── app.py              # Flask routes
├── db.py               # connection, schema, history queries
├── selection.py        # pure planning functions
├── import_data.py      # CSV to SQLite
├── backup.py           # copy workout.db to a backup folder
├── config.py           # day-type rules, priority areas, weight unit
├── requirements.txt    # flask, pytest
├── data/
│   ├── stretch.csv
│   ├── strength.csv
│   └── workout.db      # created on first run, kept out of git
├── templates/          # base, home, workout, exercises, history, summary
├── static/             # workout.js, style.css
└── tests/              # test_selection.py, test_import.py, test_app.py
```

The split keeps the selection logic free of Flask and SQLite, which is what makes it testable on its own.

## Testing

The selection rules carry the most risk, so they get the most automated tests; the screen itself is checked by hand on the real laptop and TV. Tests use pytest and a seeded random generator so results repeat.

- **Selection:** a 10-slot block over 10 areas gives one each. Extra slots reach Hips & Glutes, Thighs and Back first. The 17-slot flex block splits as described. Neck cycles through all 6 exercises before repeating while Hips & Glutes keeps serving new ones. A skipped exercise stays eligible. The second stretch block shares nothing with the first. Floor and standing exercises come out grouped. Arms & hands, with only 2 stretches, never gets more than 2.
- **Import:** blank rows are skipped, a combined area keeps its first part, capitalization variants merge, a second run changes nothing, and a row removed from a CSV becomes inactive.
- **App:** using Flask's test client, starting a Monday session creates 30 items split 10, 10 and 10; a Tuesday session creates 20 with 3 Balance; saving status and sets through the API persists; resume lands on the first pending item.
- **By hand:** Space, S, Left arrow and Enter work on the real laptop; the timer counts and beeps; images load; the page reads well when cast to the TV.

## Implementation plan

The build is 11 small steps, each ending in something runnable that is checked before the next one starts. We work through them together in the terminal, one at a time.

| Step | What we build | Done when |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 0. Setup | Virtual environment, Flask and pytest installed, folder, git init with a `.gitignore`, both CSVs copied into `data/` | `flask --version` runs and the two CSVs are in place |
| 1. Schema and import | `db.py` schema and `import_data.py` with its report | The import reports 114 stretch and 119 strength; a second run changes nothing |
| 2. Selection logic | `selection.py` and its tests | pytest passes, including the slot-allocation and rotation cases |
| 3. Plan to session | Turn a plan into a session and its items; print today's plan in the terminal | A Monday plan prints 30 items in the right block order |
| 4. Flask skeleton | Home page and the library page | The library lists all 233 exercises grouped by type and area |
| 5. Workout screen | One exercise at a time with image and text; Space, S and Left arrow | A full day can be walked through, nothing saved yet |
| 6. Saving | Status and notes saved through the API; resume | Closing and reopening the tab resumes at the right exercise |
| 7. Strength logging | Set, reps and weight rows | Sets appear in the database |
| 8. Timer | Countdown, per-side repeat, beep | A 30-second stretch counts down and prompts a side switch |
| 9. Finish and history | Finish button, per-session summary, history list | A finished session shows done, skipped and sets |
| 10. TV polish and backup | Large type, image fallback, `backup.py`, a short README | Cast to the TV is readable from the couch and a backup file is produced |

## Running and backing up

The app starts with two commands after a one-time setup, and the browser tab is cast to the TV. The commands below are for the Mac's Terminal.

```bash
python -m venv .venv
source .venv/bin/activate
pip install flask pytest
python import_data.py
flask --app app run
```

Then open `http://127.0.0.1:5000` in the browser and use the browser's cast option to send that tab to the TV.

**Backup.** All data lives in `data/workout.db`. `python backup.py` copies it with SQLite's backup routine into a folder named by an environment variable, which can point at a Google Drive, OneDrive or Dropbox folder that already syncs. The CSV export asked for as the long-term backup format is V2.

## Risks and open questions

The main risks are in the data rather than the code, and none of them blocks starting.

| Risk | Mitigation |
| --- | --- |
| Hotlinked images can disappear or block requests | Images load with no referrer and fall back to text; a later `check_images.py` can flag dead links, and V2 can download copies |
| Some rows are incomplete: Sideways walking has no dose or floor flag | The importer warns and still imports; fix in the sheet and re-import |
| Six descriptions or doses still contain copy-paste text such as "The article then directs you..." | Left as a later cleanup |
| Timer values were pre-filled from dose text, using the low end of ranges | Edit `seconds` in the sheet and re-import |
| Small areas repeat quickly: Arms & hands has only 2 stretches | Expected; adding exercises slows it down |
| Demo images belong to other sites | Personal use only; the app is never published |

**Confirmed decisions**

1. Block order on full days: standing then floor, floor then standing, standing then floor.
2. No last-logged reference on strength exercises in V1; it moves to V2.
3. Weight is in pounds.
4. The timer starts on Enter, not automatically.
5. The laptop is a Mac, so the setup commands target macOS.
6. Whole body rows with sets and reps are displayed like all other exercises: dose text, with a timer only when `seconds` is set.
